---
title: Your first Rust project
description: Create a Cargo application, add a library module, and build, run, and test it.
section: Tutorials
order: 3
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

This walkthrough creates `compute_demo`: a Cargo package with an executable, a reusable library, and one correctness test. You will add a module, call it from the executable, and follow a compiler error back to its source.

## Check the tools

[Install and load Rivet](/docs/start-here/#install-in-neovim) in Neovim 0.11 or newer. Put `cargo` and `rustc` on the editor's `PATH`. This walkthrough selects edition 2024, which requires [Rust 1.85 or newer](https://doc.rust-lang.org/edition-guide/rust-2024/index.html).

Check your toolchain in a terminal:

```sh
cargo --version
rustc --version
```

After creating the project, `:RivetDoctor` reports its Cargo tools.

## Create the project in Neovim

Start in a parent directory of your choice:

```sh
mkdir compute_demo
cd compute_demo
nvim
```

Run `:pwd` to confirm the destination, then `:RivetNew`. Select **New choices** first if a saved-profile menu appears.

| Prompt               | Choice for this walkthrough |
| -------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Project language     | `rust`                      |
| Create project in    | Current folder              |
| Project name         | `compute_demo`              |
| Git repository       | Skip Git initialization     |
| Project license      | No license yet              |
| File comment headers | No header                   |
| Project kind         | Application                 |
| Rust edition         | 2024                        |

Inspect the file tree and file previews, then press `c` or Ctrl-S to create the project. Press `q` to cancel the review. Creation writes directly into `compute_demo`.

The main files are:

```text
compute_demo/
  Cargo.toml
  .rivet.json
  .gitignore
  .editorconfig
  README.md
  AGENTS.md
  src/
    lib.rs
    main.rs
```

`Cargo.toml` describes the native package. `src/lib.rs` exports `add` and contains the test; `src/main.rs` calls that library. `.rivet.json` records Rivet's project choices and file registrations. Cargo creates `Cargo.lock` and `target/` when its commands run.

## Build, run, and test

Open the executable and build it:

```vim
:edit src/main.rs
:RivetTask build
```

Select **Debug / development** when prompted for the build choice. A successful task finishes with `Exit status: 0`.

Run `:RivetTask run`. Cargo builds and launches `compute_demo`, which prints:

```text
42
```

Run `:RivetTask test`. The library test `tests::adds_values` checks both `add(20, 22) == 42` and `add(-1, 1) == 0`. Expect one passing library test; Cargo may also report binary and documentation suites with zero tests.

Use `q` to close task output. Wait for each task to finish before starting the next.

## Add a module and use it

Run `:RivetFile`, choose **rust**, then **Source file**. Enter `number` for the name. The review should create `src/number.rs`. Approve with `c`, then open the file:

```vim
:edit src/number.rs
```

Replace its contents with:

```rust
/// Returns the example answer.
pub fn answer() -> i32 {
    42
}
```

Open `src/lib.rs` and add this declaration above its existing code:

```rust
pub mod number;
```

Cargo compiles a module after its parent declares it. Rivet creates and records the file; you choose its module declaration and visibility.

Replace the executable code in `src/main.rs` with:

```rust
fn main() {
    println!("Answer: {}", compute_demo::number::answer());
}
```

In the existing `tests` module in `src/lib.rs`, add:

```rust
    #[test]
    fn returns_answer() {
        assert_eq!(super::number::answer(), 42);
    }
```

Save with `:wall`, then run `:RivetTask check`, `:RivetTask run`, and `:RivetTask test`, waiting for each to finish. Check verifies the code without producing an executable; Run prints `Answer: 42`; Test reports two passing library tests.

## Follow a compiler error

Temporarily change `number::answer()` in `src/main.rs` to `number::answr()`, save, and run `:RivetTask check`. Cargo should report that the function cannot be found.

Run `:copen` and press Enter on the compiler diagnostic to jump to its file and location. `:cnext` and `:cprevious` move between entries. Correct the spelling, save, and run `:RivetTask repeat`; Check should succeed. Rivet uses Cargo's compiler messages and primary source spans for quickfix entries.

## Next steps

You have added, called, and tested a Rust module. Continue with [Rust and Cargo](/docs/rust/) for workspaces, extra binaries, and native dependencies. To generate API documentation from `///` comments, use [Cargo's documentation command](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/commands/cargo-doc.html).
